 ("note", "NOTE", "Living beings and biomes depend on each other: animals pollinate and disperse seeds, microorganisms recycle nutrients, humans modify landscapes for farming and cities."),
 ("h2", "10.3 Forests and People"),
 ("h3", "Forest ecotourism"),
 ("p", "**Ecotourism** is responsible travel to nature-friendly places without harming the environment while supporting local people. It stresses minimal impact, respect for communities and learning about ecosystems. The **Swadesh Darshan 2.0** scheme promotes sustainable destinations. **Thenmala** (Kollam, Keralam; 'Honey Hill') is India's **first planned ecotourism** destination, developed by the Tourism, Forest and Irrigation Departments; locals work as guides, eco-guards and service providers."),
 ("h3", "Forests and livelihoods"),
 ("ul", ["Forests give **food, fuel, fodder, medicine, shelter, timber, bamboo and firewood**.", "**Non-timber forest products (NTFPs):** honey, fruits, nuts, resins, tendu leaves, mahua flowers, lac, medicinal plants.", "Communities such as the **Gonds and Bhils** perform rituals to honour forest deities before collecting produce.", "**Sacred groves** (over 1,00,000 in India; names: *Devrai, Devarakadu, Kavu, Sarna*) are small forest patches protected for religious reasons; they hold rare plants and wildlife and help maintain streams and groundwater."]),
